"[2511.09325v1] Not Everything That Counts Can Be Counted: A Case for Safe Qualitative AI (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09325v1)\nciteturn5955view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09325v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 201\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite56†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4: cite1†Learn about arXiv becoming an independent nonprofit.†tech.cornell.edu L5: \nL6:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite2†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite3†Donate†info.arxiv.org L7: \nL8: cite57†Image: arxiv logo > cite4†cs > arXiv:2511.09325v1\nL9: \nL10: cite5†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite6†Advanced Search L11:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL12: \nL13: Search\nL14: \nL15: cite58†Image: arXiv logo L16: \nL17: cite7† †www.cornell.edu L18: \nL19: GO\nL20: ## quick links\nL21: \nL22:   * cite8†Login L23:   * cite5†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L24:   * cite9†About†info.arxiv.org L25: \nL26: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL27: \nL28: arXiv:2511.09325v1 (cs)\nL29: \nL30: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL31: # Title:Not Everything That Counts Can Be Counted: A Case for Safe Qualitative AI\nL32: \nL33: Authors:cite10†Stine Beltoft , cite11†Lukas Galke L34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Not Everything That Counts Can Be Counted: A Case for Safe Qualitative AI, by Stine Beltoft and Lukas Galke\nL36: \nL37: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L38: > Abstract:Artificial intelligence (AI) and large language models (LLM) are reshaping science, with most recent advances culminating in fully-automated scientific discovery pipelines. But qualitative research has been left behind. Researchers in qualitative methods are hesitant about AI adoption.\nL39: Yet when they are willing to use AI at all, they have little choice but to rely on general-purpose tools like ChatGPT to assist with interview interpretation, data annotation, and topic modeling - while simultaneously acknowledging these system's well-known limitations of being biased, opaque, irreproducible, and privacy-compromising.\nL40: This creates a critical gap: while AI has substantially advanced quantitative methods, the qualitative dimensions essential for meaning-making and comprehensive scientific understanding remain poorly integrated. We argue for developing dedicated qualitative AI systems built from the ground up for interpretive research. Such systems must be transparent, reproducible, and privacy-friendly.\nL41: We review recent literature to show how existing automated discovery pipelines could be enhanced by robust qualitative capabilities, and identify key opportunities where safe qualitative AI could advance multidisciplinary and mixed-methods research.\nL42: Comments: Accepted at 3rd International Conference on Frontiers of Artificial Intelligence, Ethics, and Multidisciplinary Applications (FAIEMA 2025)\nL43: Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL44: ACM classes: I.2.7; I.2.6; K.4.1\nL45: Cite as: cite14†arXiv:2511.09325 [cs.AI]\nL46:   (or  cite15†arXiv:2511.09325v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL47:   cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09325†doi.org L48: \nL49: Focus to learn more\nL50: \nL51:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL52: ## Submission history\nL53: \nL54: From: Stine Beltoft [cite17†view email ]\nL55: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 13:36:58 UTC (57 KB)\nL56: \nL57: Full-text links:\nL58: \nL59: ## Access Paper:\nL60: \nL61:  View a PDF of the paper titled Not Everything That Counts Can Be Counted: A Case for Safe Qualitative AI, by Stine Beltoft and Lukas Galke\nL62: \nL63:   * cite12†View PDF L64:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L65:   