rxiv.org L198:   * cite55†arXiv Operational Status †status.arxiv.org L199: \nL200:   *[ACM]: Association of Computing Machinery Classification\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09373v1] Routesplain: Towards Faithful and Intervenable Routing for Software-related Tasks (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09373v1)\nciteturn5955view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09373v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Software Engineering\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 8 Aug 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Routesplain: Towards Faithful and Intervenable Routing for Software-related Tasks\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Adam Štorek , cite7†Vikas Upadhyay , cite8†Marianne Menglin Liu , cite9†Daniel W. Peterson , cite10†Anshul Mittal , cite11†Sujeeth Bharadwaj , cite12†Fahad Shah , cite13†Dan Roth L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Routesplain: Towards Faithful and Intervenable Routing for Software-related Tasks, by Adam \\v{S}torek and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:LLMs now tackle a wide range of software-related tasks, yet we show that their performance varies markedly both across and within these tasks. Routing user queries to the appropriate LLMs can therefore help improve response quality while reducing cost. Prior work, however, has focused mainly on general-purpose LLM routing via black-box models.\nL17: We introduce Routesplain, the first LLM router for software-related tasks, including multilingual code generation and repair, input/output prediction, and computer science QA. Unlike existing routing approaches, Routesplain first extracts human-interpretable concepts from each query (e.g., task, domain, reasoning complexity) and only routes based on these concepts, thereby providing intelligible, faithful rationales.\nL18: We evaluate Routesplain on 16 state-of-the-art LLMs across eight software-related tasks; Routesplain outperforms individual models both in terms of accuracy and cost, and equals or surpasses all black-box baselines, with concept-level intervention highlighting avenues for further router improvements.\nL19: Subjects:  | Software Engineering (cs.SE); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2511.09373 [cs.SE]\nL21:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2511.09373v1 [cs.SE] for this version)\nL22:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09373†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Adam Štorek [cite19†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 14:42:07 UTC (203 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Sat, 8 Aug 2026 14:39:45 UTC (205 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Routesplain: Towards Faithful and Intervenable Routing for Software-related Tasks, by Adam \\v{S}torek and 7 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite14†View PDF L36:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite20†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.SE\nL43: \nL44: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L45: \nL46: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite27†cs L51: cite28†cs.CL L52: cite29†cs.LG L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite32†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: exp